<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nestorius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 386–c. 451</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Nestorius of Constantinople, Mār Nesṭōrīs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nestorius was an early Christian theologian of the Catechetical School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> who served as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> from 428 to 431. He urged cautious use of the title Theotokos for Mary, so that Christ's human and divine natures not be confused, and proposed Christotokos instead, which brought him into conflict with Cyril of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 431 the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> condemned and deposed him for heresy, and in 435 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, where he lived until about 451. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> in the Persian Empire never accepted his condemnation and reveres him as one of its three Greek Teachers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nestorius.jpg" alt="Nestorius" width="330" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dionisius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EcumenicalCouncil_3rd_09-07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sources place the birth of Nestorius around 386 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Germanicia</a>, in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. One <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> source says he was of Persian origin, while others call him a Hellenised Antiochian or Syrian. He was trained as a pupil of Theodore of Mopsuestia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and lived as a priest and monk in the monastery of Euprepius near the walls. His sermons earned him a reputation, and after the death of Sisinnius I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 428 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> enthroned him as patriarch of Constantinople.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Soon after he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, Nestorius was drawn into a dispute between two factions in Christology. One insisted on calling Mary Theotokos, &quot;God-bearer&quot;; the other rejected the title because an eternal God could not have been born. He sought a middle ground and proposed Christotokos, &quot;Christ-bearer&quot;, which neither side accepted. His opponents charged him with dividing Christ into two persons in one body; it is not clear that he taught this, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> and modern scholars believe he taught one person. Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> was the first to accuse him of heresy, and the most forceful opponent was Cyril of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><p>Cyril appealed to Pope Celestine I, who delegated to him the excommunication of Nestorius if he did not change his teachings within ten days. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> convoked a council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431. Cyril opened debate before the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> arrived, and the council deposed Nestorius and declared him a heretic. When John I of Antioch and the eastern bishops arrived, they held their own synod and deposed Cyril. The imperial government at first ordered both men deposed, and Maximianus was consecrated archbishop in Nestorius's place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 3 August 435 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> exiled Nestorius to a monastery in the Great Oasis of Hibis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He died shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451. Few of his writings survive: letters preserved in the records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, about 30 sermons, mostly in fragments, and his defence, the Bazaar of Heracleides, written in exile no earlier than 450 and surviving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was found in 1895 by American missionaries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qudshanis">Qudshanis</a> in Hakkari.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> in the Persian Empire never accepted his condemnation. The Assyrian Church of the East reveres him as a saint and rejects the name Nestorian, which Patriarch Dinkha IV repudiated in 1976. Some scholars and clergy argue that his ideas are compatible with Chalcedonian theology, though the orthodoxy of his formulation remains controversial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nestorius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nestorius</b> was consecrated archbishop of the eastern capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sixtus III became bishop of Rome four days after the death of Celestine I, who had been instrumental in the condemnation of <b>Nestorius</b> at the Council of Ephesus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-31">31 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It confirmed the original Nicene Creed and condemned <b>Nestorius</b>, who preferred to call the Virgin Mary Christotokos over Theotokos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He was instrumental in the condemnation of <b>Nestorius</b> at the Council of Ephesus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
